00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.120
For next episode, we're going
to cover orchestrator pattern,

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:58.920
review and critique pattern with
loop agent, and agent as tool.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.040
All right, let's dive in.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.670
So let's begin with the
most fundamental pattern,

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:05.990
the single agent.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.230
Imagine you want to plan a trip.

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:11.910
So with a single agent, you
want to give an instruction

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.490
on how to use a tool.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:17.210
And we can have a set of tool,
like how to check the weather,

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:19.210
check the traffic, and schedule.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.390
But for our example today,
we will simplify things

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.790
with only Google Search tool.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.470
So you will write a
comprehensive prompt

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:31.250
telling the agent how to plan
the trip using the search tool,

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:34.910
so the agent then relies on the
model's reasoning capability

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.630
to figure out the
sequence of steps.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:40.830
As you can see from the
screen, the code for this

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:44.430
is very straightforward with
ADK Agent Development Kit.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:49.310
And let's test if it's ADK
web UI by typing ADK app.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.150
So if I type, plan a
trip to San Francisco,

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:57.110
we can see in the tracing
that agent is using the search

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:01.070
tool to gather all the necessary
information in just one go.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.430
We can see how it
is using the tool

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.420
from the tracing tab over here.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:09.780
However, this works for single
task, but as you have more tools

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.140
or the tasks are
getting more complex,

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.420
for example, if I have a
request finding a sushi

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.460
in San Francisco
that's open late

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.760
and finding the fastest
way getting there.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.240
So if I build this
with single agent,

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.260
we need to define the
logic in system instruction

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.620
with this massive
prompt and the behavior

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.540
can become very unreliable.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:35.900
Since AI is non-deterministic,
you cannot always guarantee that

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.940
it will follow your multi-step
logic perfectly every time.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:45.780
So the single agent lack of
control is its main weakness.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.340
With single agent
pattern, the benefit

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.880
is it is very
simple to implement,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.580
and it's great for
straightforward, multi-step

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:54.420
tasks.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.860
However, it is less reliable
for complex workflow

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.660
and is harder to
control, and can fail

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.980
as tasks becoming more complex.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.330
Now let's get to our second
part of this video, which

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.010
is the sequential agent.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:12.330
So with the task
we covered earlier,

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.530
how do we add in more control?

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:17.370
This brings us to our
first multi-agent pattern,

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.910
the sequential agent.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:23.210
And this pattern is for highly
structured, repeatable task,

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:26.110
because the order of
the operation is fixed.

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:29.970
So the output of one subagent
becomes the direct input

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.550
for the next subagent.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:33.490
It's like an assembly line.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:37.830
And for our trip, we can break
down to two specialized agents.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:41.650
So we have this first food
finding agent and the second,

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:43.350
a transportation agent.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.410
This sequential agent
ensures that we always

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.810
run this food finding
agent first, and then

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.050
this transportation agent next.

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:55.090
So this gives us predictable,
reliable execution.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.730
And you can take
a look at a screen

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:02.890
for this code example of how to
write it in sequential agent.

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:08.090
All right, let's try
it out in ADK web UI.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:10.010
And you can look
at the tracing tab.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:13.160
You can see that it's executed
the food agent first and then

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.100
the transportation agent next.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.420
Perfect.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:19.079
So how do they communicate
with each other?

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:22.400
So they share information
through this shared session

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.980
state, which act like
a shared scratchpad.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:28.960
You can check the session
state value at this tab.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.040
And once the first agent
writes its finding,

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.680
and then the second
agent reads it from it

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.160
by using this curly braces
in its system prompt.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.840
So this is a form of short-term
memory for your agent system.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.800
And the advantages
of sequential agent,

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:50.320
is that it has high degree
of control and reliability.

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.520
It is more predictable
than a single agent,

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.920
but it can be very inflexible.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.560
This rigid predefined
structure can't

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.960
adapt to dynamic situations.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.840
Now let's go to the
third part of this video,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.440
the parallel agent.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.700
So, what if some tasks don't
need to be happen in order?

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.380
Let's say, if I want
to plan a full trip,

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.400
need to find a good museum,
find a good concert,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:16.940
and a good restaurant.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:20.440
If I'm doing them in sequential
order, it will be slow.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:23.960
This is where the parallel
agent pattern really shines.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:26.760
It allows multiple
specialized agents

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.760
to run independently
at the same time.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.260
We can have three agents
museum finder, concert finder,

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:37.640
restaurant finder, all
searching concurrently.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.440
As you can see, this
will be a lot faster

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.260
compared to doing them
in sequential order.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.900
Of course, after they
all find something,

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.900
we need to bring these
results together.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:53.880
So a common approach, is combine
this with a sequential agent.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:57.280
So first, we will run
the search in parallel.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.520
And then second, we will
run a final aggregator agent

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.600
to synthesize all the results
in a single trip plan.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:08.000
And here's the agent code
on how to put together them.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.200
[DING]

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.440
All right, let's
test in ADK web UI.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:17.910
After we type the question
from this tracing tab,

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.310
we can see three
searching agents

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:22.170
kick off all at the same time.

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:23.870
So once they're
done, the results

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:25.490
are returned to
the session state,

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:27.090
as you can see from this tab.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.990
And then the final summarizing
agent reads that state

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:32.910
and generate our plan.

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:36.870
And this logic is defined in
the system prompt over here.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.110
As you can see,
this is a great way

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:42.350
to reduce latency for tasks
that can be broken down

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:44.530
into independent subtasks.

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:47.070
So the benefit of this
design is very obvious

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:50.570
that a significantly reduced
latency by running tasks

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:51.710
at the same time.

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:54.830
However, it can have
higher initial costs,

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:58.410
because it is running
multiple agents all at once.

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:02.390
And in a lot of use cases, it
requires gather or synthesize

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:05.750
step to combine the result,
which can add complexity

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:06.910
to our design.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:09.810
All right, let's recap
what we covered so far.

00:07:09.810 --> 00:07:11.650
So we covered the single agent.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.370
It is simple to implement.

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:16.630
It can be very flexible,
but it lacks certain control

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:18.280
over this whole system.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:20.280
We also cover the
sequential agent.

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:22.180
It adds a certain
level of control

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:24.260
and make the system
more reliable,

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.560
but it is not very flexible.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.100
And lastly, we covered
the parallel agent.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:32.980
It is fast, efficient, and
great for independent tasks,

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:36.120
but it can add cost and
complexity to your system.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:37.780
With those pattern,
you can already

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:39.880
build some powerful workflow.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:41.500
And in our next
video, we're going

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:45.220
to level up with more advanced
patterns for handling even more

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:47.200
complex and dynamic problems.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:50.580
We will explore cases with
different practical examples.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.660
And the pattern we're going to
cover next episode, including

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:56.700
the loop and critique pattern
for self-correction, so

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.080
coordinated pattern
for dynamic routing,

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:02.980
and also the powerful concept
